A||||18||Lynx||What is the FASTEST!
Z||000000||Lynx||05-09-99||01:19 AM||||I have a 85 2m4 and im going to buy another Fiero becuse I LOVE them and i want one with a V6. Im just gettin tired of always hearing how fast the V6 is, so i want one. What i want too know is what year, model, and transmission had the fastest 0-60 and 1/4 mile. 
Z||000001||Eric||05-09-99||10:22 AM||||The fastest Fieros are, of course, the V6s.  Of the V6s, the cars with the 5 speed getrag transmission are the fastest. And if you really want to go fast get one with a notchback, it saves almost 200 lbs over the GT.<br>The 0-60 time for a stock notchback V6 is about 7 seconds flat.  Quarter mile in the mid 15s.  GTs get to 60 in just under 8 seconds and they do the quarter in about 16 seconds.<br>Since acceleration is only a part of what makes a sports car a performance car, I would recommend looking for one of the 88s.  For the 1988 model and the 1988 model only, Pontiac redesigned the Fiero suspension with the help of Lotus. The 88s also have larger brakes.<br>I bought a 88GT a few monthes ago(because I like the look and the options it sported) and I am getting ready to do an engine rebuild with a 3.1 crank.  With this mod and some others, I should get around 215 horsepower.  And if that is still not enough, the new pistons should have the strength to support a turbo or nitrous (stock pistons can only support about 50 extra ponies total).<br>I'll admit the 88s are hard to find and probably cost a bit more.  It is possible to make an eariler model handle as well as an 88, but you would have to spend a lot of money on aftermarket parts to do so.
Z||000002||Lynx||05-09-99||11:38 AM||||Thanks for the info. I have no problem finding 88's there are three for sale here where i live, but they are all GT's. There at a decent price one only has 88k and i could get it for $4000(Also the whole car is in great shape). Is that good or bad?
Z||000003||Eric||05-09-99||12:57 PM||||I paid $4000 for my GT with 130000 miles and had to drive it from Dallas back home to Columbus Ohio!  That sounds like a great deal.  The guy must not know what he's got.  Like I said before, the V6 has a lot of performance potential and with a few engine mods (about $500) you could be running 0-60 in less than 6 seconds!
Z||000004||EMF||05-09-99||12:58 PM||||ill  stick  with  the older notchbacks preferably 84 through 87, you can get them a ll  around here,  backyard specials, $100,$300,  3  for  $1,200, etc.  heheh ;&gt;<br>
Z||000005||EMF||05-09-99||12:59 PM||||well, i guess if  it was  84  through 87 it would  have  to be  a  notchback. duh,  my  bad.<br>they didnt make any notchbacks  in  88  right?
Z||000006||Eric||05-09-99||01:12 PM||||Also, make sure there is no frame damage and the car has a clear title.  Check the vin # too.  Make sure there isn't any outstanding recals.  It is also good for verifying that the car is indeed an 88.<br>
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||05-09-99||01:58 PM||||I used to have an 85GT V6 notchback and it was by far the fastest Fiero I have owned so far. But, like Eric said, acceleration is only part of what makes a performance car. The 85 completely lacked high-speed stability. Over 80mph it was all over the place.
Z||000008||Eric||05-09-99||03:34 PM||||The 88 notchback V6 was called the Formula.
Z||000009||Lynx||05-10-99||01:21 AM||||I live in Oregon and i looked at the new Auto trader and there was three 88'GT's for sale and they all are over $4000($4500ect) but they all say OBO so im guessing i could get them for $4000 the lowest mile one is 88K there is one up too 145K but its got a sunroof and a chip and stuff inlike the 88K. If anyone wants to read the ones for sale here look at www.traderonline.com and search under Oregon.
Z||000010||Carrolles||05-11-99||11:03 AM||||Your fastest 0 to 60 time would probably come from an 85 or 86 SE with the v-6 and  4 spd Muncie.  I can get 60 in second gear with mine.  The 5 spd Getrag needs 3rd gear to reach 60.<br>The best quarter mile times might possibly be with a properly set up automatic trans.<br>
Z||000011||Eric||05-11-99||01:43 PM||||Think about that Carolles.  The 5 speed has one extra gear so all the gears will be more closely matched.  Puts you back in the meat of the powerband when shifting.  The times are almost the same for the 5 speed and 4 speed and without an objective test, this argument will never be resolved.  <br>         -88s are better anyway-
Z||000012||Carrolles||05-12-99||09:33 AM||||What I meant about the 4spd vs the 5spd is that to get to 60 requires an additional shift ofr the 5spd.  I'm not a great speed shifter.  If I had to shift one more time to get to 60 I'd lose a lot.  * seconds doesn't give much time for extra shifts.<br>I'd love to have the Getrag 5 spd in my 85 but I'm not up for the challenge to change it out.<br>On the way back from the Daytona Fiero Festival I was traveling with two 88s.  I had to gas up more often due to my 10 gal fuel tank vs their 12 gal tank .  Also at 70 mph I'm turning around 3000 rpm vs thier 2200 rpm.<br>Take care,<br>Carroll
Z||000013||Eric||05-12-99||11:36 AM||||I've got a 10 gallon fuel tank in my 88, I think.  Could be a 12.  Are all 88s equiped with a 12 gallon tank?
Z||000014||Lynx||05-12-99||12:36 PM||||You say at 70mph the rpm's for the 5 speed is 2200. I have the old 5speed (stock 85) and at 70 its more like 2600. Also i have the stupid 13inch wheels too, If i put 15inch wheels on it will it drop the rpm's down to 2200 at 70. Dont ask me why it has the 13's it WAS manyfactured with 14's but some idiot put the base wheels on it.
Z||000015||Carrolles||05-12-99||11:15 PM||||If your stock 85 has a 5 spd it must be a 4cyl with the Isuzu trans.  This has a different final drive ratio than the v-6 with the 4 spd Muncie.  My Muncie has a 3.68:1 gear set.<br>I believe all 88s have the 12+ gal tank.<br>Carroll
Z||000016||Lynx||05-13-99||02:15 AM||||Yah its the 4cylinder with a five speed. You just said the 5speed supposed to be 2200 at 70 is that only on the 5speeds put with the V6's
Z||000017||ChadMan||05-13-99||06:55 AM||||All 88's are supposed to have 12-gallon tanks. However, I ran out of gas in my 88 Coupe the week I bought it, and when I filled it up, it would only take 9.5 gallons - even though I let it trickle until it filled up the filler neck!<p>Incidentally, I think Carrolles is right that the 4-sp V6's are slightly quicker than the 5-speeds. I have driven two 87 5-sp GTs and an 88 Formula and my 86 V6 SE 4-sp feels quicker than all of them. But I'd gladly trade half a second in the 1/4 mile for a fifth gear to shift into on long trips!
Z||000018||Carrolles||05-13-99||07:11 AM||||Lynx,<p>Yes that is only on the v-6.  I'm not sure of the gear ratios on the Isuzu 5 spd.  I do know that Ed Parks has a 4.10:1 gear set in his 85 (transplanted from an 84 I believe) and it is a very nice autocross car.<p>Take care,<br>Carroll
